

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next question is a security one, and it's forced by the code that's already there: [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m logs the username on every
  login attempt, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m receives a plaintext password. Whatever logger we build will be the thing sitting closest
  to credentials.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the logger is [3mresponsible[0m for keeping sensitive values out of logs, or whether callers are. This
  decides the logger's API shape and is genuinely hard to change later — once dozens of call sites pass raw objects,
  retrofitting redaction means auditing all of them.

  - [1mLogger redacts by key:[0m the logger owns a denylist ([38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153mauthorization[39m, …) and scrubs matching keys from
    any object logged. Buys you a safety net that holds even when a caller is careless; costs a recursive scrub on every
    call and can miss a secret stored under an unexpected key name.
  - [1mCallers redact, logger is dumb:[0m logger writes what it's given. Cheapest and most predictable; relies entirely on
    discipline and review, and one mistake ships a password to a log line.
  - [1mAllowlist fields:[0m logger only emits explicitly-declared safe fields, drops everything else. Strongest guarantee;
    most friction, and tends to make debugging harder because the field you need is the one nobody allowlisted.

  I recommend the first. A denylist scrub is not perfect, but it fails safe on the specific thing that matters here — a
  password object flowing into a log call — and it doesn't fight you the way an allowlist does when you're actually
  trying to debug something.
